  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NGA_ACJR2024 (Project)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CHAD and Plan aim to respond to the most pressing needs identified in the RNAs by focusing on three sectoral priorities: WASH services, multipurpose cash assistance (MPC) and protection services. These interventions have been designed in coordination with local and other JR partners.  

CHAD will focus on providing WASH services to affected communities, including hygiene promotion, distribution of aquatabs, water trucking, and rehabilitation of water points. They will also distribute WASH and dignity kits and provide sensitisation and awareness through mass communication, WASH committees and hygiene promoters. These activities will improve access to safe drinking water, improve hygiene and sanitary conditions in the camp and reduce the risk of disease outbreak. About 2625 HH (21,000 individuals) will be reached with these services, and they will complement CARE&#x2019;s FSL/Nutrition intervention in the same locations.  

Plan will provide monthly MPC over a 3-month period to 900 vulnerable households (7,200 individuals) to improve the nutritional status of households, particularly children under-5, pregnant and lactating women and the elderly, and to allow HHs to replace key lost assets. MPC is best suited for this crisis to stimulate markets and provide households the opportunity to utilise the resources based on needs. The monthly amount of 69,312 Naira is aligned to the local CWG.  

The project will target displaced people with protection services including identification, documentation and reunification of UASC, case management services and PSS, focussing on Child Protection to complement CARE&#x2019;s SGBV intervention.   

As a whole, the project will provide a rapid, lifesaving first response within days while at the same time adding a modest (and low-cost) element to prevent as much as possible physical and psychological short- and long-term harm.

The project will be implemented through direct implementation and local partner sub-granting. Local partner CHAD will be in charge of the WASH activities, although in close collaboration with Plan Nigeria, who will provide extensive capacity building. At this stage, CHAD is planned to receive 26% of the budget, below the 35% target due to the for-now limited capacities of CHAD. This nevertheless contributes strongly to the DRA&#x2019;s localisation principles as Plan aims to build the capacity of grassroots women-led organisations, which remain rare in Nigeria. CHAD will thus see their annual portfolio doubled by this project and may receive more funding depending on the progress of implementation. Plan and CHAD have collaborated before, and due diligence was completed in advance. CHAD was involved in the design and will be supported through trainings, close monitoring and face-to-face support of its local staff. Plan&#x2019;s role will thus be (continued) capacity building but also the rapid rollout of its own activities (Protection and MPC). 

To promote complementarity, Plan Nigeria coordinated with IFRC, JDF and CARE as well as within the existing coordination mechanisms.  Plan will complement CARE&#x2019;s GBV intervention in Teacher&#x2019;s Village Camp by providing Child Protection in those same locations, as well as in Bakassi, other camps and in the communities, while also complementing CARE&#x2019;s FSL and nutrition activities in MMC and Jere through WASH activities in the same locations.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Heavy rainfalls are causing severe flooding all across northern Nigeria, so far affecting 31 states and over 1 million individuals. Borno State in the northeast is among the most affected states, with over 414,000 people severely affected, 400,000 people displaced, 37 people reported dead, and 58 people injured. When the recent flash floods broke out in Borno State, northeast Nigeria was already facing an acute food crisis and an ongoing conflict with non-state armed forces. The flash floods in Borno state, caused by heavy rainfalls and aggravated by the breaking of the Alau Dam in Konduga Local Government Area (LGA), have caused extensive damage to infrastructure, crops and shelter, severely impacting livelihoods and displacing households.

According to the National Emergency Management Agency (NEMA), the fast-moving waters submerged over 70% of Maiduguri city. OCHA estimates that over 400,000 people in Borno State were displaced, forcing them to relocate to former and operational IDP camps already housing IDPs. Former and operational IDPs camps quickly reached full-capacity, unable to provide safe-drinking water, food, and basic NFIs to recently displaced populations.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SUMMARY
The Borno State flooding took place in September 2024 in Borno State, Nigeria, after the collapse of the Alau Dam on 10 September. The Maiduguri and Jere local government areas were particularly affected: according to the National Emergency Management Agency, over 70% of the residents in Maiduguri were displaced. On the basis of RNA findings, Plan International and CHAD will provide WASH services to respond to the risks of waterborne illnesses and the lack of safe and clean water, multi-purpose cash (MPC) to meet the multisectoral needs caused by the crisis (due to a lack of food but also diverse NFIs where people have lost their belongings), and protection services to respond to the high protection risks identified among in particular the displaced population, including children separated from their families, children unable to attend school, and women/girls at risk of SGBV.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project will prioritize vulnerable groups in the flood-affected communities, including female and child headed households, separated and unaccompanied children, women, children and adolescents with special needs, women, children and adolescents at risk of abuse, exploitation and GBV, and IDPs.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">When the Alau Dam was breached in the night of 9-10 September, eventually displacing at least 400,000 people according to OCHA, Plan Nigeria was just conducting an RNA to respond to floods further south. The scope was immediately adjusted, and an extensive RNA was completed between 12th and 15th September in the affected areas of MMC, Jere and Mafa.

Individuals included in Plan&#x2019;s RNA had within days already been relocated across former and active IDP camps throughout the three LGAs, including the Teacher&apos;s Village Camp (TVC) and Bakassi camp. The government now aims to close all &#x2018;former&#x2019; camps in the next few weeks, thus returning flood-affected people to their communities, despite Plan&#x2019;s RNA finding that WASH facilities are poor both in the targeted IDP camps and within communities, where sanitation systems have been damaged, putting populations at high risk of waterborne illnesses. Access to food is extremely limited, with the main market in the LGAs (Monday Market) interrupted in an already acute food crisis. The sudden displacement has left women, girls and boys in a particularly dire situation, with heightened risks of psychological distress, SEA, and GBV, as well as separation from family members.</narrative>
  </description>
